y we observed a significant  number  of serum protein electrophoresis only  orders  and those comprised 139  of the approaches in the primary care  setting  the total number of protein  electrophoresis orders  uh that lacked the recommended serum  free light chain studies comprised more  than 80  of encounters in the primary care  setting and so this was worrisome  to me as a laboratory director  and on a broader scale 63 percent  of all non-oncology orders were  comprised of test combinations  without recommended free light change  studies or in some cases urine studies  now if you compare my system to the  findings of ginzan  remember he found that 72 percent  of the respondents were not  adherent to guidelines in 2018 um  perhaps our system performed a little  bit better than  than that group but still there was  certainly  opportunity for improvement in our  system  so what i have found working with  clinicians  is that encouraging adherence to a  diagnostic test guideline  if that's an end into itself really  isn't perceived by clinicians as a  motivating force  but on the other hand what really should  be motivating to all of us in healthcare  is the opportunity to reduce the time to  diagnosis  so using a model originally published at  the 2017 american society for hematology  annual meeting  we theorized here in our system that  there would be a significant reduction  in the time to diagnosis  from 149 days with my current  system with my system's current pattern  of utilization  down to 45 days if following the  recommended guidelines  and really this difference could  positively impact  avoidance of downstream complications  and their associated costs things like  chronic renal impairment  infections and neurologic or bone  disease things that tend to  cost a lot of health care dollars  so um affecting change  looks differently at every institution  and the laboratory is going to be  positioned  differently in different institutions  different systems  at my institution significant changes  to the laboratory formulary or other  impactful changes are first vetted  through the clinical laboratory advisory  committee  or we like to call clack for short  this is a multi-disciplinary  physician-led committee that's been  around for about two years  and this group is responsible for  promoting and facilitating test  optimization opportunities in a fiscally  responsible manner  so when we launched this committee we  ensured  that it reported up the leadership chain  within the organization  to the medical executive committee and  had the backing of the medical executive  committee  so in addition to reviewing and  approving order sets containing  diagnostic tests  other examples of our past projects have  included  restriction of q24 hour laboratories  prevention of duplicate  once-in-a-lifetime genetic tests  and then the creation of new order panel  cascades  this might be the the best slide in the  presentation honestly  what we created for consideration for  clack were three new epic order panels  and the goal of these order panels was  to guide providers in the direction  of the optimal guideline compliant test  selections  these panels were initially vetted by  hematology specialists  as well as our cardiology specialists  and they had reached out to me  specifically  requesting an amyloid evaluation panel  to be used in their heart failure clinic  so our clinical laboratory advisory  committee approved these proposed panels  with an initial launch that occurred  in the summer of 2020. so i want to  point out some key features  of these panels for the screening panel  which is depicted at the top of the  slide  we opted to continue with serum protein  electrophoresis with reflex  to immunofixation as opposed to default  ordering  and ife study for all the screens  we clearly stated on the myeloma screen  panel the sensitivity of that  combination of tests  for all plasma cell disorders which  is over 94 percent and really 100  if you're just considering multiple  myeloma we wanted to provide that  information really to reassure providers  that this was an  optimal combination of tests  if you look down at the amyloid  evaluation panel  we included urine studies and that was  on purpose to maximize sensitivity  of detection and then if you look down  at the  monitoring panel for myeloma we made  urine studies an  unchecked option uh in the follow-up or  monitoring setting  now if you go back all the way to the  one of the first slides i showed you and  you the concept of the nudge  um i want to go back to that because we  built these panels without restricting  choice  uh in other words all the tests can  still be ordered  individually by the clinician and the  pre-checked options can be modified  uh at any time the text selections are  clear to the provider and it's really  easy to opt out and that's really  key in the nudge you want to make it  transparent and easy to opt out  so that you preserve provider choice  so it would have been nice if the  expected modifications had been  immediately successful however  we encountered implementation challenges  those included  things like difficulty finding all the  epic locations where  non-optimal test orders were located in  addition to the fact epic has a  functionality now  where physicians can create their own  preference lists within epic so you when  you're trying to do search and replace  functions  with test orders it can be very  challenging  the other thing we recognize was that  despite calling out the new panel  options  we wanted to market them and let  physicians know that they were available  and we did that through our regular  physician communication channels  there was still despite the  communication a high likelihood  that the updates we were sending were  were going to be missed by physicians in  their inboxes  so ultimately we discovered that despite  our effort  all the appropriate preference lists we  originally assumed had been updated in  august of 2020 in fact  had not been updated the updates  had only occurred on inpatient ordering  contacts with an epic  not the ambulatory list which were  actually more crucial for the success of  our project  and the way we discovered this was by  closely monitoring for  some expected impacts so first we looked  for  impacts to test volumes we were looking  for things like  fewer serum protein electrophoresis only  orders  or increased free light chain orders  second we partnered with our epic order  specialist  to create an order panel utilization  report  using the clarity database that's built  within epic monitoring that report  became very helpful for us because it  confirmed to us that the new panels were  only being used in the inpatient setting  which was very odd  to me so it prompted us to take quick  action  to address the problem and ask the  orders team whether or not the  outpatient  ordering contacts have been updated with  the appropriate with the appropriate  panels  so i wanted to show you an example of a  custom report that we generated using  epic slicer dicer if you're unfamiliar  with epic  slicer dicer is a self-service query  tool that's built within epic  and it allows users like me to have  quick view into patient populations  the data are pulled from the back end of  the epic data warehouse  so what this report demonstrates and i  apologize  that the axi axes are so small and you  can't really see but what it's  demonstrating  is the monthly number of encounters in  which there is a protein electrophoresis  order  concurrent with a serum free light chain  order  and the timeline goes from august of  2020 that's the first data point  all the way to april of 2021 the last  data point  and so what we have observed is a very  significant uptick in the monthly number  of orders that are considered guideline  compliant  and that change really flipped for us  between february  and march of this year  we also monitor a monthly order panel  utilization report again  as i mentioned generated automatically  via the epic clarity database  this monthly report actually is emailed  it comes to my inbox every month  and we can clearly see if you're looking  at the left-sided graph  the uptick in utilization of the myeloma  screening order panel  and and this is uh the time frame that's  depicted here is from january of 21  all the way to april 21.  on the other hand if you look at the  right side that's the amyloidosis  evaluation panel utilization  it remained rather stable throughout  that same time period  and that's because our impact had  happened earlier  we had quickly been able to get uptake  of utilization with the small group of  cardiologists because  they were engaged they had asked for the  panel and they began using it  immediately so we had impacted change  back at the end of 2020 so  for 2021 we just demonstrate stability  of utilization by that group  if you look at overall utilization of  all our new order panels  and i don't have this depicted on the  slide but it went from 24 uses in  october of 2020  to 81 total uses during the month of  april of 2021 and that represents an  over 200 percent increase in utilization  of our new panels  and this slide further demonstrates our  success  uh particularly the success in the  primary care setting  in 2018 2019 that was the initial time  period  for the the data that we  evaluated 80 percent i mentioned this  earlier 80  of our evaluations lacked the serum free  light chain studies  we've been able through our efforts to  reduce that percentage down to 32  percent  lacking serum free light chains and  early this year  another thing we've looked at is serum  protein electrophoresis  only orders those orders went from 11  percent of all diagnostic workups in  2020  down to only 3 percent in early 2021.  so in conclusion our audit findings  highlighted a gap  in test order optimization  here in our system we leverage various  tools in epic  however where you are you may have  another electronic medical record or  laboratory information system and i'm  sure they have similar tools  that make the investigative work much  easier  so with the availability of tools like  the ones i've shown  identifying opportunities quite honestly  is the easy part  guiding change however is a much more  challenging position to be in  so really active engagement with  clinician leaders  via a laboratory stewardship committee  like the one we have here is a very very  important strategy  as the laboratory medical director it's  also  very important to cultivate  relationships with other health system  leaders  leaders like the chief information  officer your chief medical information  officer your chief  quality officer and others these  relationships  strengthen the laboratory position and  particularly when you're trying to make  changes with your health system you have  to have the buy-in  if you're changing clinical practice or  making changes with the  emr you've got to have buy-in from your  other clinical and administrative  leaders within your system  and i'll leave you with one last  take-home message  and that is as laboratorians we are in a  position  of privilege that is we have line of  sight  to the ordering behavior of all our  clinicians in our system  and as a response to that we have the  responsibility  to leverage our unique position to drive  conversations about the patterns we  observe  and to nudge health care providers again  nudge health care providers  to optimal test choices  so with that i really want to move into  the q a time and happy to answer any  questions you might have  thank you dr hart for that informative  presentation  we are now going to start the live q a  portion of the webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  and we'll answer as many of your  questions as we have time for  so dr hart let's get started in your  study  you cited primary care as your target  improvement area  how did other clinical specialty areas  compare from a compliance perspective  so that's a very good question and one  we were also  interested in because we we do know that  these type studies are used by diverse  specialties  the nephrologists in our system  neurologists in our system  also frequently are orders of  these panels of tests so um  in the abstract that we published  we specifically called out our  nephrology and neurology specialists  they were slightly more compliant with  the  the test the optimal test guidelines i  think  if i recall the nephrologists were 26  compliant with guidelines and the  neurologists  were 17 compliant so still  quite a gap it was a little bit more  difficult for us to  understand compliance for  the hematologists oncologists because we  weren't entirely sure when we evaluated  the data set we had to make the  assumptions as i mentioned in my talk  that they were mon they were ordering  primarily for the purpose of  confirmation  or for monitoring and so for  confirmation for example  if an outpatient primary care evaluation  included you know a limited test or a  serum protein electrophoresis only  and the the uh oncologist followed up  with a free light chain study it  appeared in the data set to look like it  was just a free light chain only order  so it was very a little bit more  difficult for us to make definitive  conclusions  about the patterns of test ordering on  the oncologist  great thank you so much dr hart moving  on to our next question  how long would you say it took your  health system to implement this project  so if i were to go back through um  my email inbox and try to find the very  first time  that i initiated a a request to our it  here it probably if i added up all the  months  it would be probably 12 to 15 months and  that seems like  a lot but it quite honestly could have  been longer and i i know from prior  experiences that these projects can tend  to drag out  long long long periods of time and and  it becomes very frustrating for the  laboratory because you  expect to see progress and you don't see  it and you don't know why you see it  so um i would say that  for our system uh we have a  very collaborative relationship between  our epic  i.t folks and our laboratorians and so  through regular meetings through emails  phone calls  we're able to move things through very  quickly now i  i did mention in the talk that we did  vet this  uh project through uh leadership here in  our health system and that that can  add a little bit of a delay but our our  laboratory advisory committee meets  uh every other month and med exec meets  every month so honestly  those are very reasonable timelines i  think for for implementing projects  um the hard part  uh the thing that that took a lot  quite a bit of time was um  getting the utilization reports built  and so we we had to go back and forth  with our epic order specialist to create  that custom report so that i could  really have a line of sight to who was  using  our newly created panels and so  that that honestly can be a quite a bit  of time can elapse for systems  where um you know you're wondering what  the response is and  you're looking for an unexpected  response and you don't see it  and so getting the custom report built  so that we  knew you know so that we could really  evaluate clearly  uh what the impact uh was  um and getting that that report right  uh was probably the the the  the biggest challenge for us  understandably and you know going back  to a time just a follow-up question you  know  what were the most time-consuming  elements of it  yeah i think getting that that custom  utilization report  that was probably the the longest step  and really you know monitoring for  efficacy you just can't neglect that  part and  and we would go back and forth and i  think i mentioned in the talk that we  we first um it took months a couple  months for us to realize that the only  providers who were using the new panels  were inpatient providers  and really the inpatient setting is not  where  the majority of these workups are  happening and we were not seeing  outpatient providers use them and we  wondered why is that  and so i had to go back and forth with  our epic orders team and we had to  figure out where the order panels  had been placed what preference lists  they were on which  preference lists were they not on and so  that's probably the most time consuming  part was figuring out are these order  panels  in the proper location within the emr so  they get seen  by the providers you want to see them  thank you so much dr hart and we want to  thank our audience for all these amazing  questions that are coming in  so dr hart our next one what do you  think are the most effective ways for  laboratories to influence  clinician behavior  yeah that's a very good question um  going back to the concept of the nudge i  think  that's a very important concept because  um  when we don't see the results that we  want to see  it might be um our knee-jerk reaction  might  not be to nudge but to complain or you  know  not go about it in the optimal way i  think nudging clinicians  in the right direction can involve  sometimes some subtle things  um like again building the choices  within the electronic medical record  in such a way that  um it's it's not  not difficult for a clinician to do the  right thing  and so that i think that that order  panel design  uh naming of of tests  those are maybe indirect ways but  they're very important ways that  that the laboratory can influence uh  clinicians  clearly forming relationships  is crucial and so when i first came to  to my current role within the first  six months that i was here i made a  point to visit every single  department head in our system and to go  to their office and to  have time and sit down with them and ask  them what  were their you know what were their  concerns from a laboratory perspective  and forming those relationships from the  very start  of my tenure here was very very  important i think has  has positioned the laboratory in a very  positive  light in our health system  so dr hart our next question is a  two-part question for you  first portion is can you comment on your  involvement with epic  in getting the panels up and running and  the second portion  do you have any additional words of  wisdom  sure so to get those panels  up and running um  i initially created  again what the panels looked like what  tests  uh were included in the panels gave them  a name  um then again they were vetted by the  clinical laboratory advisory committee  and on that committee  we have representation from both the  inpatient  uh epic team and the ambulatory epic  team  so once they were vetted  by the advisory committee  and then approved by medical executive  committee  i it really was back and forth  between me and the epic team making sure  that the the  or new order panels were on the  appropriate preference lists  and then working directly with the epic  team in order to create the custom  reports that we needed in order to  monitor for  uptake and efficacy  as far as additional words of wisdom  i would say one of the things we have  tried  to do here and um so i meet monthly with  our chief information officer  and early on  i tried to convince him that  um the  laboratory was a gold mine of data and  so  um i see the mission of i.t  and the mission of the laboratory to be  very similar  and that you know the laboratory has  tons of data  the electronic medical record has tons  of data and we're trying to  to uh evaluate the data  and and then derive insights from the  data  and then um  present the data to make decisions and  so  what we've done here is really try to  integrate  uh both our lis team uh the epic orders  team  and there's a bunch of different it  teams our bi team  uh we've really tried to integrate with  the lab in fact recently  this was within the last two years we  hired a clinical lab data analyst  who um you know we hired to be  to sit within the laboratory and yet  that individual  has a direct line of connection to the  epic  bi team and so that further cemented the  relationship between  the i.t side and the laboratory side  quite honestly the laboratory is the  only department within our health system  who's chosen a model like that with  regard to data analytics  so dr hart our next question for you is  a two-part question as well  you mentioned difficulty in creating  awareness of the new order options  what avenues did you use to communicate  the new order options  and the second part was it email only or  were there other communication tools  your hospital uses to  communicate such changes  yeah so this is a great question and  it's a challenge for any health system  how do you get  important information to very busy  clinical providers and so we actually  opted  uh in this case to stick with the  regular communication channel that we  have in our system to communicate to  providers and that is through a weekly  email flash that goes out so  all of the content related to these new  order panels we sent out via  this regular email update  now recognizing that  communication is a challenge that's  where the  design of the order panel build and how  it's placed in the emr  becomes very important because with a  really well designed nudge  you may not even require  some official marketing campaign or  communication  uh because you've created you positioned  the new order panel in such a way that  it becomes hard not  to use it and so in a day and age where  providers are so uber busy and  communication is a challenge  really that order panel design and how  you place it in the emr becomes even  more important and so the second half of  that question was  uh about whether we used email  i think i answered that yes so so yeah  it was primarily email  great thank you so much dr hart and  before we let you go for the day we've  got one final question for you  do you have any advice for other health  systems out there who are looking to  implement  a project like this  yeah um be patient and  and and don't give up  uh honestly the hard part is not  the getting the panel out there it's  the hard work of monitoring for efficacy  so you  you know you've got to do that part  because you're you you can't really  improve  without making sure that what you have  done has had the  intended impact and so that can take  months  and and in our case uh  it did take additional months of  figuring out  what the implementation roadblocks were  but by doing that and  really looking for expected impacts then  ultimately you can get to where you you  hope to be  thank you again dr hart for your time  today and for your important research  we'd also like to thank labyrinth and  our sponsor binding site  for underwriting today's educational  webcast before we go i'd like to thank  the audience for joining us today  and for their interesting questions  questions that we did not have time for  today  and those submitted during the odd  demand period will be addressed by our  speaker via the contact information that  you provided at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand  laborats will alert you via email when  it's available for replay  we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event  until next time thank you for joining  bye everyone
 

